{"parentUuid":"4580298c-bd01-4728-a21d-4c6ff23c127c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"64d71192-3b1b-4e70-aef9-d69f40568796","agentId":"ab56e030d7ec24cad","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014uZK1fDeeoXjzKUHHeCw2s","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CPMpy 2026 release MUS QuickXplain PyPI version\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"cpmpy · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/cpmpy/\"},{\"title\":\"Explanation tools (cpmpy.tools.explain) — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/simpl_bool_fix/api/tools/explain.html\"},{\"title\":\"Releases · CPMpy/cpmpy\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/CPMpy/cpmpy/releases\"},{\"title\":\"Solving with assumptions — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/latest/unsat_core_extraction.html\"},{\"title\":\"UnSAT core extraction with assumption variables — CPMpy 0.9.21 ...\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/stable/unsat_core_extraction.html\"},{\"title\":\"UnSAT core extraction with assumptions — CPMpy 0.9.23 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/linearize-power/unsat_core_extraction.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - CPMpy/cpmpy: Constraint Programming and Modeling library in Python, based on numpy, with direct solver access. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/CPMpy/cpmpy\"},{\"title\":\"UnSAT core extraction with assumptions — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/complex_sudoku_examples/unsat_core_extraction.html\"},{\"title\":\"Installation instructions — CPMpy 0.9.23 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/latest/installation_instructions.html\"},{\"title\":\"cpmpy\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/cpmpy/0.9.13/\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found about CPMpy, MUS (Minimal Unsatisfiable Subsets), QuickXplain, and PyPI versions:\n\n## CPMpy QuickXplain and MUS Implementation\n\nCPMpy includes a `quickxplain()` function that finds a preferred minimal unsatisfiable subset of constraints, based on the ordering of constraints. The QuickXplain implementation supports solvers that must support assumptions, such as \"ortools\", \"exact\", \"z3\" or \"pysat\".\n\nCPMpy also includes the MARCO algorithm for enumerating minimal unsatisfiable subsets (MUSes) and minimal correction sets (MCSes) of unsatisfiable constraints.\n\n## Current PyPI Version\n\nBased on the PyPI information available, the current version shown is CPMpy 0.10.0, though documentation references also indicate versions like 0.9.24 and 0.9.23.\n\n## 2026 Release Information\n\nThe search results do not contain any specific information about a CPMpy 2026 release. The most recent release information available shows version 0.10.0 on PyPI, but there is no information about a scheduled 2026 release with specific features related to MUS or QuickXplain.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"fc11714f-d523-4d35-a183-ad620b4eb4dd","timestamp":"2026-05-09T23:36:51.705Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4580298c-bd01-4728-a21d-4c6ff23c127c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/zhuran24/claude-pj/zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.138","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"6b922e81-8eea-42eb-9ea2-d05af2f8eb6c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"64d71192-3b1b-4e70-aef9-d69f40568796","agentId":"ab56e030d7ec24cad","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NmSsoNfhtRcQMgdEPgyXQz","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SMT-COMP 2025 results QF_LIA QF_BV winners\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"SMT-COMP 2026\",\"url\":\"https://smt-comp.github.io/\"},{\"title\":\"Qf_slia Single Query | SMT-COMP\",\"url\":\"https://smt-comp.github.io/2023/results/qf-slia-single-query\"},{\"title\":\"SMT-COMP | The International Satisfiability Modulo Theories (SMT) Competition.\",\"url\":\"https://smt-comp.github.io/2023/benchmarks.html\"},{\"title\":\"SMT-LIB The Satisfiability Modulo Theories Library\",\"url\":\"https://smt-lib.org/news.shtml\"},{\"title\":\"Local Search For Satisfiability Modulo Integer Arithmetic Theories | ACM Transactions on Computational Logic\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3597495\"},{\"title\":\"SMT-COMP 2025\",\"url\":\"https://smt-comp.github.io/2025/\"},{\"title\":\"Qf_lia Single Query | SMT-COMP\",\"url\":\"https://smt-comp.github.io/2021/results/qf-lia-single-query\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) SMT-COMP: Satisfiability modulo theories competition\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/225778702_SMT-COMP_Satisfiability_modulo_theories_competition\"},{\"title\":\"| SMT-COMP 2024\",\"url\":\"https://smt-comp.github.io/2024/results/largest-contribution-single-query/\"},{\"title\":\"Qf_bv Incremental | SMT-COMP\",\"url\":\"https://smt-comp.github.io/2021/results/qf-bv-incremental\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, SMT-COMP 2025 results are available as of August 11, 2025. However, the search results don't contain the specific winners for the QF_LIA and QF_BV categories. The search found the main SMT-COMP 2025 page but didn't retrieve the detailed results for those particular logics.\n\nTo find the specific winners for QF_LIA and QF_BV in SMT-COMP 2025, you would need to visit the official SMT-COMP website directly at smt-comp.github.io and navigate to the 2025 results section, where the detailed competition results for each logic are typically published.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"69c74d0e-cac7-4460-89d4-334b255c4930","timestamp":"2026-05-09T23:36:51.904Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6b922e81-8eea-42eb-9ea2-d05af2f8eb6c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/zhuran24/claude-pj/zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.138","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"007be426-c824-4f8d-bcdb-b22dcdd36645","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"64d71192-3b1b-4e70-aef9-d69f40568796","agentId":"ab56e030d7ec24cad","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NThn3ubdiWhi24SzvEuGBE","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"cvc5 release 2025 2026 Python binding SMT-LIB performance\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"(PDF) cvc5: A Versatile and Industrial-Strength SMT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/359546891_cvc5_A_Versatile_and_Industrial-Strength_SMT_Solver\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - cvc5/cvc5: cvc5 is an open-source automatic theorem prover for Satisfiability Modulo Theories (SMT) problems. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/cvc5/cvc5\"},{\"title\":\"Releases · cvc5/cvc5\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/cvc5/cvc5/releases/\"},{\"title\":\"cvc5 1.3.1 on PyPI - Libraries.io - security & maintenance data for open source software\",\"url\":\"https://libraries.io/pypi/cvc5\"},{\"title\":\"Quickstart Guide — cvc5 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cvc5.github.io/docs/cvc5-1.0.2/binary/quickstart.html\"},{\"title\":\"cvc5: A Versatile and Industrial-Strength SMT Solver | Tools and Algorithms for the Construction and Analysis of Systems\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1007/978-3-030-99524-9_24\"},{\"title\":\"cvc5: A Versatile and Industrial-Strength SMT Solver | NSF Public Access Repository\",\"url\":\"https://par.nsf.gov/biblio/10388056-cvc5-versatile-industrial-strength-smt-solver\"},{\"title\":\"cvc5: A Versatile and Industrial-Strength SMT Solver | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-99524-9_24\"},{\"title\":\"cvc5 · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/cvc5/\"},{\"title\":\"Cooperating Validity Checker - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cooperating_Validity_Checker\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about cvc5:\n\n## Overview\n\ncvc5 is the latest SMT solver in the cooperating validity checker series and builds on the successful code base of CVC4.\n\n## Python Bindings\n\ncvc5 features APIs for several different programming languages such as Python and Java. More specifically, cvc5 has bindings for C++, Python, and Java. The latest version available on PyPI is version 1.3.3 (uploaded February 25, 2026).\n\n## SMT-LIB Support and Performance\n\nThe primary input language for cvc5 is SMT-LIB v2. cvc5's performance on all benchmarks in SMT-LIB has been evaluated and compared against CVC4 and Z3.\n\n## Recent Releases (2025-2026)\n\nThe search results show that cvc5 continues to be actively developed with releases in 2026. Recent updates include improvements to proof formats and various new features, though the specific details of 2025-2026 release notes focus on technical enhancements rather than comprehensive release information.\n\nFor more detailed information about specific 2025-2026 releases, performance benchmarks, and latest features, I'd recommend checking the official cvc5 GitHub repository releases page or the project documentation at cvc5.github.io.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"a1a67ad9-8166-4ac9-8d38-1c39d3283790","timestamp":"2026-05-09T23:36:52.543Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"007be426-c824-4f8d-bcdb-b22dcdd36645","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/zhuran24/claude-pj/zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.138","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
